---
title: Dev inbox
name: Dev inbox
description: A local mailbox that catches every email your app sends in development, so you can read the real HTML instead of a console dump — no config, no second tool.
category: Guides
---

Mail you send while building has to go somewhere. Sending it for real means a stray
customer address is one typo away; printing it to the terminal means you never see the
thing you're actually building.

So Postboi ships a local inbox. Start your dev server and mail lands in a mailbox at
`/__postboi` instead of going out — rendered HTML, headers, attachments and all.

**Your code doesn't change.** No `preview()`, no `if (dev)`, no separate provider. The same
`mail()` call that sends in production is captured in development.

## Vite projects — nothing to run

If you have the [`postboi/vite`](/cloudflare-workers#the-config-file) plugin in your
`vite.config.ts` (`postboi init` adds it), the inbox is already there:

```bash
bun run dev
```

```
  ➜  Local:    http://localhost:5173/
  ➜  Postboi:  dev inbox at http://localhost:5173/__postboi
```

It rides on the dev server you already started — no extra port, no second terminal. That
covers SvelteKit, Astro, Nuxt, Remix and plain Vite.

Because it's mounted by Vite's `configureServer` hook, which only ever runs in dev, the
inbox cannot end up in a production build.

## Everything else — `postboi dev`

Express, Hono, Next.js and bare `wrangler dev` have no Vite to hang it off, so run the
inbox yourself:

```bash
bunx postboi dev
```

```
  Postboi dev inbox: http://localhost:1080/__postboi
  Mail from this project is captured here instead of being sent.
```

It advertises its port in `node_modules/.postboi/`, so an app started in the same project
directory finds it with nothing configured. Running somewhere else — another directory, a
container, a different runtime — set `POSTBOI_INBOX` to the port:

```bash
POSTBOI_INBOX=1080 bun run dev
```

## Just looking at it

`--demo` fills the inbox with sample mail — a styled HTML message, a FormData table with
an attachment, and a text-only body — so there's something to look at without wiring an app
to it:

```bash
bunx postboi dev --demo
```

It re-seeds on every start, which also makes it the way to work on the inbox itself: run it
under a file watcher and an edit restarts the server with the sample mail still there.

```bash
bun --watch src/cli/index.ts dev --demo
```

## What gets captured

**Everything**, including a provider with real, working credentials. That's the point: a
laptop should not be able to mail a real person because a test fixture had a real address
in it. With the inbox running, `mail()` never reaches a provider.

Two guards keep that from ever biting a deploy:

- It only happens when `NODE_ENV` is exactly `development`. An unrecognised environment is
  treated as production, so nothing is ever silently swallowed on a server.
- If the inbox isn't reachable — you killed the dev server, the port moved — the message is
  **printed to the console**, never sent. A send you thought was captured can't quietly
  become real mail.

> **On an HTTPS dev server**, the inbox is served over HTTPS too — it's mounted on that same
> server — and Postboi works this out for itself. Its certificate doesn't need to be one
> anything trusts. If you're pointing at an inbox by hand, `POSTBOI_INBOX` takes a whole URL
> as well as a bare port:
>
> ```bash
> POSTBOI_INBOX=https://localhost:5173 bun run dev
> ```

## Turning it off

To send for real from your machine, pick whichever fits:

```ts
// postboi.config.ts
import { config } from 'postboi'

export default config({
	dev: { inbox: false },
})
```

```bash
POSTBOI_INBOX=off bun run dev
```

```ts
// vite.config.ts — don't serve it at all
postboi({ inbox: false })
```

## Reading the mail

The inbox lists messages newest-first and shows each one four ways:

| Tab             | What it shows                                                       |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Message**     | The HTML, rendered in a sandboxed frame — scripts blocked, as a real client would |
| **Plain Text**  | The `text` part, including the one [auto-derived](/config) from your HTML |
| **Source**      | The raw HTML, for when a provider mangles something                 |
| **Attachments** | Every file, downloadable, with its type and size                    |

Above them: `from`, `to`, `cc`, `bcc` and `reply_to` exactly as the provider would have
received them — after defaults are applied and addresses parsed. If `reply_to` isn't what
you expected, this is where you find out.

Messages live in memory for the life of the dev server. Restarting it empties the inbox.

### The windows are real windows

The mailbox and the reader are the desktop's own windows, not the app's: **drag** them by
the title bar, **resize** from the right edge, bottom edge or the corner grip, **maximise**
with the box in the title bar or a double-click, and **minimise** to the taskbar. Clicking a
taskbar button restores a window — or minimises it if it's already focused, as the real one
did.

They don't belong to Postboi Local. Minimise the app and your mail windows stay where they
were; bring one back from the taskbar and it comes back on its own, with no application
frame behind it. The app itself resizes and windows the same way, and it always sits behind
your mail — focusing the frame can't bury the message you were reading.

**Minimise** Postboi Local and the mail windows go down with it, so what you get is the
desktop rather than a hole in a pile of windows — and restoring brings back exactly the ones
that were up. Minimise or close one on its own and the rest are unaffected.

**Close** Postboi Local and it takes its windows with it and leaves the desktop. The
**Postboi** shortcut sitting there opens it again on a double-click, and it drags around
like any other icon.

That matters more than the nostalgia: the reading pane is only ever big enough because you
can make it bigger. **Prev** and **Next** at the bottom of the reader step through the
mailbox without going back to the list.

### Outbox, Sent, Scheduled, Deleted

The list is an **Outbox**, not an inbox — these are messages on their way out, caught before
they went. Four folders, and the tabs carry their counts:

| Folder        | What's in it                                                     |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Outbox**    | Everything still going out — Sent and Scheduled together.          |
| **Sent**      | Went the moment it was sent, or its scheduled time has passed.     |
| **Scheduled** | Sent with [`scheduled_at`](/scheduling), still ahead of its time.  |
| **Deleted**   | A scheduled send you called [`cancel()`](/scheduling#cancelling-a-scheduled-send) on. |

```ts
const { id } = await mail({
  to: "ada@example.com",
  subject: "Reminder",
  body: "<p>See you tomorrow.</p>",
  scheduled_at: { days: 1 },
})

await cancel(id)   // moves it to Deleted
```

Without this, a mail queued for next Tuesday would sit in the list looking exactly like one
already gone — and one you'd cancelled would look like it was still going out, which is the
one answer that could actually mislead you. Opening a message says so again at the top.

Leave the tab open across a scheduled time and the message moves from Scheduled to Sent, with
a noise to say so. **Nothing is really queued**: the inbox captured these instead of sending
them, and the only clock involved is the one in the page.

> **Rescheduling** (`mail.messages.reschedule`) is a Postboi-provider API call, not a send, so
> it doesn't pass through the inbox — a captured message keeps the time it was captured with.

### Sound

New mail announces itself. There's a greeting on the way in, a sign-off when you close
Postboi Local, a different one for turning the computer off, and a noise when a scheduled
message reaches its time. The **Sound** button in the toolbar mutes the lot, and the choice
is remembered, because a dev tool that says "Welcome!" in an open-plan office is a dev tool
people uninstall. Pressing **SIGN ON** is what lets any of it play — see below.

### Signing on

The inbox opens on a Sign On dialog. The credentials are set dressing — there's nothing to
authenticate against — so the fields are filled in and disabled. Press **SIGN ON** and it
dials in: _Locating mailroom… Connecting to localhost… Intercepting outgoing mail…_ — each
panel filling in as its step is reached — then greets you and hands over the desktop.
**Cancel** skips straight through.

It isn't only theatre. Browsers refuse to play audio until the page has been interacted
with, so on a cold load the handshake and the greeting would never be heard. Pressing SIGN
ON is that interaction, which is what makes the sound work at all.

The inbox is loaded and listening the whole time, so nothing is waiting on the dialog.

### Configuring the defaults

The sound and the sign-on can each start off, for everyone on the project:

```ts
// vite.config.ts
postboi({ inbox: { sounds: false, intro: false } })
```

```bash
bunx postboi dev --no-sound --no-intro
```

These set what the page *starts* with. The toolbar toggle still works, and a viewer's own
choice is remembered and wins — so muting the project doesn't stop anyone turning it back on
for themselves. `inbox: false` disables the whole thing.

### Yes, there's a Start menu

Press the **Windows key** (**Cmd** on macOS) and the Start menu opens: your mailbox, Check
Mail Now, Delete All Mail, links out to the dashboard and the docs, the sound toggle, and a
**Turn Off Computer** that does exactly what you'd hope. Only a bare press counts — held as
a modifier it's left alone, since that's how you copy and reload.

### The desktop

Minimise everything and there's a desktop behind it, with a postman running across it. The
wallpaper is baked into the package; the clip is not — it streams from Mux the first time
the inbox is opened, because an encode small enough to publish looked like a photograph put
through a fax machine.

That is the one thing here that reaches the network. If it can't — you're offline, or the
machine doesn't allow it — nothing breaks and nothing is logged: the wallpaper is the clip's
opening frame, so all you lose is the postman. **No mail ever leaves your machine**; the
capture path has nothing to do with this.

## Tests still use the mock

The inbox is for reading mail with your eyes. In tests, keep using
[`postboi/mock`](/providers#mock-provider), which captures to `sent` with no server
involved:

```ts
import Mock from 'postboi/mock'

const mail = new Mock({ default: { from: 'no-reply@example.com' } })
await mail.send({ to: 'contact@example.com', subject: 'Hi', body: '<p>Hello</p>' })

expect(mail.sent).toHaveLength(1)
```

> The inbox stands in front of **sending** only. `mail.lists`, `mail.contacts` and the other
> [Postboi provider](/provider) namespaces still talk to the real API in development —
> managing an audience isn't something you'd want faked.
